<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">North-West Frontier Province</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1901">1901</a>–<a href="/years/2010">2010</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Khyber Pakhtunkhwa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The North-West Frontier Province was a province created by the British in 1901 from the frontier districts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a> as its capital. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">Princely states</a> such as Swat, Dir, Amb and Chitral lay alongside it, and the tribal areas beyond. It became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> in 1947, absorbed the princely states in 1969 and was renamed Khyber Pakhtunkhwa in 2010.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/north-west-frontier-province.jpg" alt="North-West Frontier Province" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MS05L; Original uploader was Pahari Sahib at en.wikipedia; derivative work by MS05L · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_NWFP.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> annexed the north-western frontier after the Second Sikh War of 1848–49, and the region remained part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> until the province was created from its north-western districts in 1901. Commonly known as Sarhad, the frontier, it served with the Frontier Tribal Areas as a buffer with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. Until 1947 it bordered five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> to the north, the minor states of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgit-agency">Gilgit Agency</a> to the north-east, the Punjab to the east and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balochistan">Balochistan</a> to the south. It covered 70,709 square kilometres in six divisions, Bannu, Dera Ismail Khan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hazara">Hazara</a>, Kohat, Mardan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a>, to which Malakand was later added.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In a referendum held in July 1947, before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/partition-of-india">Partition of India</a>, the province voted overwhelmingly to join <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, and it acceded on 14 August 1947. The chief minister, Dr Khan Sahib, with his brother Bacha Khan and the Khudai Khidmatgars, boycotted the vote because it offered neither independence nor union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>.</p><p>In 1955 the province was merged into the new province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-pakistan">West Pakistan</a> under the One Unit policy announced by Prime Minister Chaudhry Mohammad Ali, and its offices of governor and chief minister ended on 14 October 1955. It was recreated in 1970, when the One Unit system was dissolved, and kept its name until 2010, when the Eighteenth Amendment to the Constitution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> renamed it Khyber Pakhtunkhwa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>At independence there was a clear Muslim Pashtun, Hindkowan and Punjabi majority, with significant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> minorities scattered across the province. Most people spoke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashto">Pashto</a> or Western Punjabi, chiefly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindko">Hindko</a> and Saraiki, and Kohistani was also spoken; before the British, Persian had been the official language. The tribal areas were counted separately in the colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">censuses</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 1911 described <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> as less strictly observed in the southern districts, such as the Derajat, than in the north, through which the Khyber and Kurram routes had long carried conquerors, and found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> of the province less particular about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> than those further east. Colonial census reports also noted how hard it was to tell Hindus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> apart.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “North-West Frontier Province” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Since 2001 the army has fought the Tehrik-e-Taliban Pakistan in <b>Khyber Pakhtunkhwa</b>; by its own reckoning the war on terrorism has cost up to $118 billion, over 81,000 casualties and 1.8 million displaced civilians.</blockquote><a href="/empire/islamic-republic-of-pakistan">Islamic Republic of Pakistan · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…fragments from ancient Buddhist monasteries in northwest China, Afghanistan and northwest Pakistan, among them the Spitzer Manuscript of c. 200 CE from near Kizil and the birch-bark scrolls of the Bajaur Collection, of the 1st to 2nd century CE, found in 1999 at a site in <b>Khyber Pakhtunkhwa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/arthashastra">Arthashastra · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the British, Amb was a small internally autonomous state on the <b>North-West Frontier</b>, with the Nawab ruling the trans-Indus enclave while the family held Tanawal as an estate in British Hazara.</blockquote><a href="/state/amb-state">Amb State</a></li><li><blockquote>Its princely status was abolished in 1969, when it became a district of Pakistan's <b>North-West Frontier Province</b>, now Khyber Pakhtunkhwa.</blockquote><a href="/state/chitral-state">Chitral State</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1960 Nawab Shah Jahan Khan was deposed, and in 1969 the state was abolished and became a district of the <b>North-West Frontier Province</b>, later divided into Upper and Lower Dir.</blockquote><a href="/state/dir-state">Dir State</a></li><li><blockquote>Under British rule it was a very small autonomous holding on the <b>North-West Frontier</b>, governed by a Khan over a Muslim population speaking Hindko.</blockquote><a href="/state/phulra-state">Phulra State</a></li></ul></section>
